127 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 70 0 R
/T (Least Recently Used \(LRU\) Page Replacement Algorithm  The least recently used page replacement algorithm keeps the track of usage of  pages over a period of time. This algorithm works on the basis of the principle  of locality of a reference which states that a program has a tendency to access  the same set of memory locations repetitively over a short period of time. So  pages that have been used heavily in the past are most likely to be used heavily  in the future also. )
/E (Least Recently Used \(LRU\) Page Replacement Algorithm  The least recently used page replacement algorithm keeps the track of usage of  pages over a period of time. This algorithm works on the basis of the principle  of locality of a reference which states that a program has a tendency to access  the same set of memory locations repetitively over a short period of time. So  pages that have been used heavily in the past are most likely to be used heavily  in the future also. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
128 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 70 0 R
/T (In this algorithm, when a page fault occurs, then the page that has not been used  for the longest duration of time is replaced by the newly requested page. )
/E (In this algorithm, when a page fault occurs, then the page that has not been used  for the longest duration of time is replaced by the newly requested page. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
